= Distributed cognition — Technical =

== Functional-system boundary ==
Distributed cognition expands the analytic unit from individual actor to a system linked by functional relations.

Representational state can propagate across:

<pre>
person → artifact → person → environment → person
</pre>

The transformations across media are part of the cognitive process under analysis.

== Representational propagation ==
External representations differ from internal ones in persistence, visibility, manipulability, and access.

These properties alter error detection, memory burden, coordination, and computational sequence.

== Temporal distribution ==
Cognition can also be distributed through time.

Procedures, records, conventions, and designed artifacts preserve results of prior cognitive work for later actors.

== Method ==
Cognitive ethnography combines observation, artifact analysis, interaction analysis, and representational modeling to reconstruct real-world cognitive systems.

== Boundary caution ==
A broad system boundary should earn explanatory value.

Merely using a tool does not automatically make every task a useful distributed-cognition case.


== Functional analysis and cognitive ethnography ==
Hutchins's navigation studies and the framework of Hollan, Hutchins and Kirsh (2000) treat the transformations of representations across people and artifacts as part of the cognitive process. In a navigation team, one participant measures a bearing, another plots it on a chart, and the chart constrains the subsequent estimate of position. A trace of the representations at successive interfaces can identify where errors enter, which operations can be done in parallel, and how a persistent external record coordinates actions across time. This analysis concerns a particular function and boundary; it does not require attributing all cognition to the group.

== Experimental and design methods ==
Combine naturalistic observations and interaction transcripts with controlled task comparisons. Change one property of an external representation, such as visibility or persistence, and measure downstream accuracy, coordination latency, interruption recovery and memory burden. Compare a shared display with independent displays or an individual baseline while matching information availability. The observed benefit might reflect access, lower transcription costs or reduced working-memory demand rather than a change in individual intelligence. Study breakdowns as well as successful episodes to identify causal dependencies. In a digital knowledge environment, preserve representation provenance and distinguish shared state from synchronized belief.

== Neighboring concepts ==
Group cognition focuses on interaction-level meaning-making; collective intelligence often concerns performance; situated cognition emphasizes context; dispersed knowledge concerns where relevant knowledge resides. They are distinct analytical questions. Distributed computation can implement a coordination architecture without establishing that the system is cognitive in a human or phenomenological sense.

